-- #1182: two DIFFERENT interfaces each declare a method named `m`. Which one runs is
-- decided by the DECLARATION ORDER of the two `impl` blocks. Swapping them (control.mdk)
-- changes no program meaning and changes the answer.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS FIXTURE DOES NOT ASSERT THAT 1 IS WRONG AND 2 IS RIGHT, OR THE REVERSE. Which
-- value is correct is genuinely in dispute (see the issue: scoping the candidate set by
-- interface vs. reporting the collision are both live options). The ASSERTABLE DEFECT is
-- the ORDER DEPENDENCE: main.mdk and control.mdk differ only in the order of two `impl`
-- blocks, and they must not disagree. Today they do, silently, at exit 0, on both engines.
data Q a b c = Q a b c

interface A1 a where
  m : a -> Int

interface A2 a where
  m : a -> Int

impl A1 (Q Int y z) where
  m _ = 1

impl A2 (Q x Bool z) where
  m _ = 2

main = println (m (Q 1 True 7))
